* text=auto eol=lf

# The demo rig is CAPTURED AUDIO AND MODEL DATA, and its bytes are the measurement.
#
# ⚠️ `-text` FOR EVERY FILE IN IT, INCLUDING THE `.nam`s THAT HAPPEN TO BE JSON. The captures were made from a
# real amplifier and cabinet; re-encoding a `.wav`, re-sampling it, or re-serialising a `.nam` through an editor
# that normalises line endings makes every number ever measured about this rig describe bytes that no longer
# ship. Git must store them exactly as they are, on every machine, whatever `core.autocrlf` is set to.
#
# `-diff` is the other half: a 300 KB JSON file diffed as text produces a useless blob of numbers on every
# touch, and there is nothing in these files a reviewer reads in a diff.
assets/demo-rig/* -text -diff

# The WordPress plugin is PACKED, and the zip's SHA-256 is an update-channel contract (scripts/pack-wp-plugin.mjs).
#
# ⚠️ `eol=lf` SO A WINDOWS CHECKOUT PACKS THE SAME BYTES AS EVERY OTHER MACHINE. Measured 2026-10-12: the
# Windows CI lane and a Windows install from a git checkout both packed 0.9.14 to 203,370 bytes / e17dd0f4,
# while macOS and Linux packed 202,420 bytes / 05bb75db — the same source, with `core.autocrlf=true` turning
# every LF into CRLF on checkout. Once the manifest for a version is published, the packer's deliverability
# check refuses any build whose hash differs, so every `npm run build` on such a checkout would fail exactly
# the way the `zip` shell-out made it fail. The packer also turns CRLF back into LF (packedBytes in
# scripts/lib/wpPluginRelease.mjs) for checkouts made before this pin, and verify-plugin-pack.mjs refuses a
# package carrying CRLF.
wp-plugin/morpheus/** text eol=lf
